1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,864
Three months ago, Aziz printed a ticket at a copy shop.

2
00:00:03,914 --> 00:00:08,208
He logged in to the marketplace website on the shop's computer, and he never logged out.

3
00:00:08,258 --> 00:00:13,560
Last week, he bought a new phone and installed the mobile bank, with a green TAF check.

4
00:00:13,610 --> 00:00:22,272
Today, a stranger at the copy shop opens that old browser, finds the list of sessions, and taps End on the mobile bank on Aziz's new phone.

5
00:00:22,322 --> 00:00:25,680
Two sessions of one person, and one wants the other gone.

6
00:00:25,730 --> 00:00:27,552
Which one should win?

7
00:00:27,602 --> 00:00:31,704
In Lesson 21, each session got its tokens and its level.

8
00:00:31,754 --> 00:00:37,440
This lesson adds two more facts to every session: when it started, and its rank.

9
00:00:37,490 --> 00:00:39,600
First, the rules that fail.

10
00:00:39,650 --> 00:00:43,296
Rule one: any session can end any other session.

11
00:00:43,346 --> 00:00:46,512
Then the stranger ends the owner, and keeps the door.

12
00:00:46,562 --> 00:00:49,296
Rule two: the older session wins.

13
00:00:49,346 --> 00:00:54,384
That sounds fair, but the copy-shop session is three months older than Aziz's new phone.

14
00:00:54,434 --> 00:00:56,256
So the stranger wins again.

15
00:00:56,306 --> 00:00:59,256
Age alone cannot tell the owner from the thief.

16
00:00:59,306 --> 00:01:03,120
Something else must count: how strongly the session was checked.

17
00:01:03,170 --> 00:01:04,992
That is the rank.

18
00:01:05,042 --> 00:01:09,984
A session has rank two when it is in the master app and its TAF verdict was green.

19
00:01:10,034 --> 00:01:15,216
It also has rank two when the verdict was yellow and the customer then passed MyID.

20
00:01:15,266 --> 00:01:22,608
When TAF was down and nothing was scored, the session has rank one, until a later green verdict raises it.

21
00:01:22,658 --> 00:01:28,584
Every other session has rank one: every web session, and every session in a side app.

22
00:01:28,634 --> 00:01:30,456
Now the rule.

23
00:01:30,506 --> 00:01:34,896
A session can end another session only when that session has a lower rank.

24
00:01:34,946 --> 00:01:38,448
Or when both have the same rank, and the other one is younger.

25
00:01:38,498 --> 00:01:40,320
Back to the copy shop.

26
00:01:40,370 --> 00:01:42,744
The stranger's session has rank one.

27
00:01:42,794 --> 00:01:45,024
Aziz's new phone has rank two.

28
00:01:45,074 --> 00:01:49,800
So the platform refuses, and writes an audit event and an antifraud event.

29
00:01:49,850 --> 00:01:53,448
Now Aziz opens the list of sessions in the mobile bank.

30
00:01:53,498 --> 00:01:57,120
He sees the copy-shop browser, with its app and its start time.

31
00:01:57,170 --> 00:02:01,704
He taps End, and it ends, because rank two outranks rank one.

32
00:02:01,754 --> 00:02:04,296
Age matters only between equals.

33
00:02:04,346 --> 00:02:10,560
Two marketplace sessions, both rank one: the older one can end the younger one, but not the other way around.

34
00:02:10,610 --> 00:02:12,624
I think this is a quiet, good idea.

35
00:02:12,674 --> 00:02:15,504
The owner usually has the strongest check.

36
00:02:15,554 --> 00:02:18,240
A thief usually has the oldest forgotten door.

37
00:02:18,290 --> 00:02:21,912
So the rank comes first, and the age only breaks a tie.

38
00:02:21,962 --> 00:02:23,904
One more rule cuts deeper.

39
00:02:23,954 --> 00:02:30,744
A password change or a password reset ends every other session of the same or a lower rank, whatever its age.

40
00:02:30,794 --> 00:02:35,496
The age rule applies only when the customer ends one session from the list.

41
00:02:35,546 --> 00:02:37,632
Lesson 30 uses this.

42
00:02:37,682 --> 00:02:44,328
Each new session also sends a notification: by push to every other session, and by SMS.

43
00:02:44,378 --> 00:02:46,944
So no new door opens in silence.

44
00:02:46,994 --> 00:02:49,872
And there is a second guard: the probation period.

45
00:02:49,922 --> 00:02:56,016
A fresh session is the riskiest moment, even after a green TAF verdict and a MyID check.

46
00:02:56,066 --> 00:02:57,816
A check can be fooled once.

47
00:02:57,866 --> 00:03:04,776
So after a new session, a new phone, or a password reset, each app blocks some of its own actions for a while.

48
00:03:04,826 --> 00:03:07,104
Which actions, and for how long?

49
00:03:07,154 --> 00:03:10,824
The bank decides, for each app, in the platform's policies.

50
00:03:10,874 --> 00:03:13,872
The platform does not block those actions itself.

51
00:03:13,922 --> 00:03:15,744
It does not even know them.

52
00:03:15,794 --> 00:03:24,768
Instead, the token of each session carries four facts: when the session started, when its probation ends, its rank, and its level.

53
00:03:24,818 --> 00:03:30,624
The backend of each app reads them, and blocks its own risky actions until the probation ends.

54
00:03:30,674 --> 00:03:37,296
So the mobile bank and the marketplace can each be careful in their own way, with one shared truth about the session.

55
00:03:37,346 --> 00:03:40,896
To recap: every session has a start time and a rank.

56
00:03:40,946 --> 00:03:46,176
Rank two comes from the master app, with a green verdict, or a yellow verdict and MyID.

57
00:03:46,226 --> 00:03:49,776
A session ends only lower ranks, or younger equals.

58
00:03:49,826 --> 00:03:53,184
A password change ends all same or lower ranks.

59
00:03:53,234 --> 00:03:58,368
And the probation slows down every new session, in each app's own way.

60
00:03:58,418 --> 00:04:00,528
Now picture a sharper stranger.

61
00:04:00,578 --> 00:04:04,416
He talked the mobile operator into a new SIM card with Aziz's number.

62
00:04:04,466 --> 00:04:09,360
He installs the mobile bank, and asks for a new password, as if he forgot it.

63
00:04:09,410 --> 00:04:11,544
Aziz still has active sessions.

64
00:04:11,594 --> 00:04:13,704
Should that reset happen at once?

65
00:04:13,754 --> 00:04:15,936
Next: the password reset.
